The reviewer's "strongest remaining objection" names a single recurring theme:
**dispatch policy is a per-call boundary, not a per-action one.** That theme was
previously recognised only on the network axis (round 7) and not applied
consistently elsewhere. It is now stated once as a general rule and applied to every
affected capability, rather than patched case by case.

FIX 1 (CHECK 5) — the proxy binding is now part of the release gate.
  - P1c `ACCEPTANCE_CRITERIA` (6) changed from "criteria 1–7" to "**criteria 1–8**",
    and the `PROXY_SPAWN_BINDING` row was **explicitly renumbered as criterion (8)**
    so the gate and the requirement are visibly the same thing. The row is no
    longer a descriptive aside that the gate does not reference.
  - `SECURITY_TEST_VECTORS.md §4` gained a **column B0 — "proxy present, NOT
    bound"** — where every row that is `ASK` in column B reads `DENY`. Column B was
    relabelled "proxy present **and bound**". This makes the distinction testable:
    an unbound proxy is not an enforcement point, so `ASK` there would be the same
    false guarantee the whole `ASK`/`DENY` split exists to prevent.
  - NET-03 (DNS) now reads `DENY` in **every** column unless resolution is pinned
    *through the bound proxy*, since resolver pinning depends on the binding.

FIX 2 (CHECK 6) — PE-06a′ was over-claimed, in exactly the way PE-06c already was.
  - The row now reads `DENY` **with uid separation AND a mode-protected inode**;
    `KNOWN_LIMITATION` otherwise. A different uid cannot read a mode-protected file
    it does not own, but it **can** read a world-readable one — the same reason
    PE-06c resists uid separation.
