ROUND 12 — FIXES APPLIED
================================================================================

Round 12 returned REJECTED on five checks (3, 5, 6, 11, 12) and rejected three of
the seven claimed fixes as PARTIAL. The two halves of that verdict are the same
finding seen from two directions, and the author accepts both.

The reviewer's structural objection: the submission had *introduced* a vocabulary
and then *not applied it*. F1 was PARTIAL because the table said `NOT_ENFORCED`
and the explanatory bullets in the same section still said `DENY`; the vocabulary
was also miscounted ("three values" over four). F5 was PARTIAL because the
admission-stage split was made and the contradictory `OUTCOME_STRICT` declaration
was left standing above it. In other words, round 11's own lesson — that a repair
which is not propagated is not a repair — was itself only half-applied. The
author's post-round-11 claim that the consistency sweep had caught the residual
9e(b) instance was therefore accurate but *not sufficient*: it was a sweep over
the wrong set of files.

FIX 1 (new defect — vocabulary miscount). `COMPONENT_REUSE_MATRIX.md §4` said
  "three-valued" over a table of four. Corrected, and `ALLOW` was added as an
  explicit row rather than left as the unglossed default in the capability table:
  `ALLOW` is only available where a named confinement layer permits the effect on
  every surface, which is a positive claim and needed the same "what must exist for
  it to be written here" column the other three already had.

FIX 2 (new defect — F1 was PARTIAL). The explanatory bullets under the §4 table
  still read "`DENY` for the same effect attempted inside a running shell". They now
  read `NOT_ENFORCED`, name the realistic consequence the vocabulary requires
  (a sandboxed shell may signal siblings it can see and may exec any workspace
  binary — within the stated posturing, not a boundary escape), and cite the round
  that introduced the residual.

FIX 3 (CHECK 3 — the session-lifecycle contradiction, previously an ACCEPT).
  The reviewer reversed an earlier ACCEPT, which is the correct outcome: P5
  criterion 1 said mode is "never resolved from ... session state" while
  `REFERENCE_ARCHITECTURE.md §10` requires a per-session activation that expires
  on session end. Those cannot both hold and no mechanism was named.
  The reconciliation is a distinction the documents were collapsing:
    - **excluded absolutely**: any model-reachable or MCP-writable session state;
    - **the mechanism §10 requires**: a non-persisted activation held in the
      *runner's own process state*, keyed to the host's session lifetime, created
      only on presentation of the single-use token.
  P5 criterion 1 now enumerates exactly two host-owned sources and forbids
  everything else; `REFERENCE_ARCHITECTURE.md §10` gained a **Transition owner**
  paragraph naming the runner/server lifecycle as the owner of every arrow in its
  state machine. Both documents now name the same owner, and the ambiguity of the
  word "session" is removed rather than left to the reader.

FIX 4 (CHECK 5a — peer credentials are not a spawn identity). F4 was PARTIAL
  because the preferred capability form was still "a socket whose peer credential
  is checked by the proxy". `SO_PEERCRED`/`LOCAL_PEERCRED` yields a **uid and a
  pid**, not a spawn: every child of one runner shares the uid, so the first child
  to connect takes the grant — the exact failure criterion 8 exists to prevent,
  restated. A pid is not a substitute (reusable after reaping, and observable
  rather than unforgeable). The criterion now names three *sufficient* forms —
  pidfd-keyed handoff, a socket the profile permits only on the spawning process
  and refuses to inherit, or a per-spawn secret over a non-forwardable channel —
  and states that a backend offering only a peer-credential check yields
  `NOT_ENFORCED` per-spawn attribution, hostname grants stay unavailable, and that
  is a recorded backend limitation rather than something to work around.

FIX 5 (CHECK 5b / CHECK 12 — the B0 column contradicted column A). B0 set every
  `ASK` row to `DENY` while annotating the cells "same as column A", on rows where
  column A is `ASK`. The reviewer's deeper reading is the correct one: a broken
  *optional* proxy cannot be the stated reason an **independently compiled
  literal-IP rule** stops applying. The corrected column is the identity
  **B0 = A**, justified per row: literal-IP rules come from the compiled sandbox
  network profile and are proxy-independent; hostname rules need a per-spawn
  enforcement point, which an unbound proxy is not. This is a *stronger* claim
  than the one it replaces — it asserts an unbound proxy is worth nothing in every
  row, rather than worth "a denial" — and it is testable as an identity.

FIX 6 (CHECK 6 / CHECK 12 — `OUTCOME_STRICT` was false on the target platform).
  §2 declared `OUTCOME_STRICT` for "all of PE-01…PE-12 with the single documented
  exception of PE-06a'" while the same section stated PE-06a is a macOS limitation
  and PE-06b/PE-06c added after admission are not enforced. PE-06 is now excluded
  from the family declaration, and each member carries a per-member outcome naming
  surface and admission stage, in a table. The rationale is stated: `OUTCOME_STRICT`
  is a property a vector can be *tested against*, so it may only be claimed where a
  single expected outcome exists on every surface. A specification that cannot
  accept or reject an implementation is not a specification.

FIX 7 (CHECK 11 — CP-04's proof method was underspecified). The method was
  accepted; the closure condition was not. A call graph with missing edges
  establishes nothing, because an absent edge reads as "no path" — the conclusion
  being claimed. CP-04 now enumerates seven edge categories (static vtables with
  open impl sets, function-pointer registration, cross-module closures, `#[cfg]` /
  target-conditional code, unexpanded macros, IPC/plugin re-entry, generated code)
  and requires each to be resolved or recorded as a residual. Edges take a
  **three-valued** verdict — `REACHABLE` / `UNREACHABLE` (proved) / `UNKNOWN`
  (undecidable) — and a site passes only when all paths to it are `UNREACHABLE`.
  The same `NOT_ENFORCED` discipline as §4: the forbidden move is converting "I
  could not decide" into "it does not happen". The required *output* is specified
  (machine-checkable edge set, per-edge verdict, unresolved-category list) because
  a hand-written summary is not an acceptable artifact for a security gate.

