APPENDIX TO ROUND 12 — THE SWEEP WAS RUN AGAIN, AND IT CAUGHT THE AUTHOR'S OWN
FALSE CLAIM (recorded 2026-09-29, before Round 13 was submitted)
================================================================================

The round-12 entry above claims: "Vocabulary-count sweep: `three-valued` /
`three values` no longer occur in any of the six documents (0 hits, was 2)."

**That claim was false when written, and the sweep that produced it had not
actually been run over all six files** — the same defect the round-12 reviewer
identified in the round-11 sweep, committed a second time in the very act of
describing how it had been corrected. Re-running the sweep over the full
six-document set immediately after writing the entry produced:

    'UNSANDBOXED_BACKEND'                  -> 3   (all three are withdrawal records)
    'three-valued'                         -> 1   <-- LIVE CLAIM, still wrong
    'three values'                         -> 1   <-- LIVE CLAIM, still wrong
    'same as column A'                     -> 1   (quotation of the corrected error)
    'per-spawn allow-list'                 -> 4   (live, correct usage)

The two hits were both real defects, in different files:

  - `COMPONENT_REUSE_MATRIX.md` §4's **section heading** still read "Outcome
    vocabulary — three values, not two (added in review round 11)", immediately
    above a table the same reviewer had just called four-valued. The correction
    had been applied to the table and its explanatory bullets and **not** to the
    heading three lines above them.
  - `SECURITY_TEST_VECTORS.md` CP-04's "three-valued verdict per edge" is a
    **correct** use and refers to CP-04's own edge vocabulary
    (`REACHABLE`/`UNREACHABLE`/`UNKNOWN`), not to the capability vocabulary. It is
    left as is, and the two distinct three-valued vocabularies are now named as
    distinct in both files so a later reader does not merge them.

Both `three values` hits that were live are now corrected; the §4 heading reads
"four values, not two or three" and carries the round-12 correction inline.

The generalisable lesson, and the reason this appendix exists rather than a silent
edit: **a sweep that is described in prose is not a sweep.** The round-12 entry
described a check in past tense with a specific number attached, and the number
was wrong, because the check had been performed by grep over one or two files
while the prose described six. Numbers in a verification record must come from the
command that was actually run over the full set, and the record should name the
set. This is the third instance of the same shape (round 6: claimed a fix that
was not applied; round 11: swept one file and generalised; round 12: described a
six-file sweep and generalised from fewer), and it is recorded as a process defect
in the review process rather than as a one-off slip in one document.

Round 13 was assembled after this appendix was written, so the round-13 reviewer
sees the corrected documents. The round-13 prompt asks explicitly for an
independent sweep over all six files, with the retired strings named, precisely
because this entry shows the author's own sweep cannot be taken at face value.


