  the two *assessed* alternatives fail on facts (crate unpublished, sibling stale)
  that the unassessed ones are not variations of, and both unassessed options would
  still have to be built before reaching the adapter's position. The gate is
  explicit: P0 does not begin profile compilation until pinned-git and
  bounded-vendoring have each been assessed against the same four criteria, and if
  either comes out equal-or-better the recommendation is **reopened**, not defended.

FIX 2 (CHECK 3 — "session end" had no workable boundary). Round 12 named a
  transition owner; round 13 correctly observed that keying to the runner *process*
  lifetime leaves the ordinary case unanswered — an individual logical session
  ending while the runner stays alive. `REFERENCE_ARCHITECTURE.md §10` now tabulates
  three distinct lifetimes (logical session / runner process / TTL+revoke), states
  what each clears, and names the mechanism: the activation is keyed to **the
  logical session id the runner already mints** for the activating request — an id
  the runner owns and the model never supplies. The honest fallback is stated rather
  than assumed away: a runner with no session-end signal expires on **TTL only**,
  and per-session expiry is `NOT_ENFORCED`. P5 criterion 1 was updated to match.

FIX 3 (CHECK 4 — the single-function contradiction). `REFERENCE_ARCHITECTURE.md §4`
  said the sandbox "must ultimately be attached at a single normalized spawn
  function" and then specified a two-part surface three lines later;
