ROUND 14 — FIXES APPLIED
================================================================================

Nine blocking checks, and only T10/T11 accepted as claimed. The author's reading of
this round is that it exposed something the previous fourteen had not: **a fix that
makes a claim *narrower in the wrong dimension* is not a fix.** Three instances, and
they are the same mistake:

  - FS-06/FS-07 were `run_shell` vectors. Round 13 made their strictness apply "on
    the structured surface only" — a surface those vectors never exercise. The
    qualifier became a claim about nothing.
  - CP-04's process-entry inventory said "every binary, script, hook, and lifecycle
    step invocable from a model-reachable tool". A shell can name any path, so that
    set is not finite and the inventory claimed something it cannot deliver.
  - `secret.read` was corrected in round 13 from "the whole capability" to "the
    enumerated list". Round 14 showed the list-scoped `DENY` was itself
    surface-scoped: the profile denies the secret's *path*, and a hardlink reaches
    the same inode by another name.

In each case the previous round's repair converted an over-claim into a *differently
shaped* over-claim, and the reviewer's phrase for it — "a strict qualifier on a
surface the vector does not reach is not a weaker claim, it is a claim about
nothing" — is the most useful sentence produced in fourteen rounds. It is recorded
here as the round's lesson, because rounds 15+ should apply it directly: **when a
claim is too strong, check whether narrowing the qualifier changes what the claim is
about. If it does, the qualifier was the defect.**

FIX 1 (CHECK 1 — the gate existed in one document and not the other). §2 promised
  P0 would not compile a profile until pinned-git and bounded-vendoring were
  assessed, and P0 contained no such criterion. **P0 criterion (11) now exists** and
  names both options, the same four criteria, the recorded verdict, and the
  consequence: if either is equal-or-better on all four, the `SUBPROCESS_ADAPTER`
  recommendation is reopened and P1b does not start against it.

FIX 2 (CHECK 3 — P5 still defined "session" as the process lifetime). The clause
  survived at the tail of P5 criterion 1. It now says the logical session tabulated in
  §10, explicitly **not** the runner process lifetime, and keeps the TTL-only
  fallback with per-session expiry marked `NOT_ENFORCED` — described as a disclosed
  fallback, not as a claim of session-end revocation.

FIX 3 (CHECK 4 — §6 still attached the backend at a single site). §6 said the trait
  is "attached inside `ManagedChild::spawn`", contradicting the §4 pair. It now says
  the trait is applied pre-`exec` **at the sandbox-attach surface defined in §4**,
  names both sites, and states that a caller of the trait that is neither is an
  uncovered spawn site that fails CP-01/CP-02.

FIX 4 (CHECK 5 — the relay case; the reviewer's strongest objection, accepted in
  full). Round 13's "sufficient" list offered a pidfd-bound accept-time peer check.
  That answers "which process opened this connection?", and the threat is "**whose
  traffic is this?**". Spawn A, holding a legitimate grant, can accept a connection
  from B and forward B's bytes out through A's own authorised socket; the proxy sees
  a peer it recognises, on a connection it authorised, carrying bytes it cannot
  attribute. **Rounds 12, 13 and 14 have now each produced a plausible-sounding
  mechanism — inherited fd, pidfd, accept-time peer check — that controls who can
  *present* while the threat is who can *ride*.** Criterion 8 is rewritten around
  that: the property needed is **traffic attribution**, deliverable by provenance at
  the syscall boundary (platform-specific, not assumed available), by removing the
  relay surface in the profile (preferred — removes the attack rather than detecting
  it), or by endpoint-anchored grants the proxy terminates itself. If none is
  available, `NOT_ENFORCED` per-spawn attribution and hostname grants stay
  unavailable — now the **primary** branch, not a fallback, because three rounds have
  shown what a fallback produces. The negative test gained the leg that was missing:
  (1) B claims to be A, (2) B is handed A's descriptor, (3) B connects as A, and
  **(4) B hands its traffic to A and A relays it**. Legs 1–3 are identity tests; a
  mechanism that passes all three still failed in round 14.

FIX 5 (CHECK 5b — B0's condition was not established by P1c). B0 = A is now
  conditional on the profile denying children any route to the unbound proxy, and the
  reviewer's further point is adopted: the condition must hold for **every separately
  granted literal-IP route**, not only the proxy's own address — otherwise a granted
  IP is a second egress while the hostname rows still read `DENY`.

FIX 6 (CHECK 6 — FS-06/FS-07). Strictness is **withdrawn** from these vectors rather
  than relocated. The round-13 platform split is also corrected: the post-admission
  hardlink residual is **platform-independent**, not macOS-only, so the expectation is
  now keyed to *how the outside file is addressed* — direct outside path ⇒ `DENY` on
  both platforms; the same file reached through a link created after admission ⇒
  `NOT_ENFORCED` on both.

FIX 7 (CHECK 8 — `secret.read` was list-scoped but not surface-scoped). The cell now
  reads `DENY` for the enumerated locations and patterns **by path, on the structured
  surface**, with a third `NOT_ENFORCED` case added: an enumerated secret **reached
  by another name**, because a path-name denial does not deny the content. The
  principle is stated: the floor is a floor over a **list**, and on the shell surface
  it is a floor over **path names**.

FIX 8 (CHECK 10 — "managed path and nothing else" was unsupported). §2.1a records
  that the direct `Command::spawn()` sites **also** set process groups, so tree
  ownership already extends past `ManagedChild::spawn`. The cell now says tree
  ownership is largely present on **both** paths, that what is missing is a single
  enforcement point, and — adopting the reviewer's structural point — that **inheritance
  of confinement is a separate question this row does not answer**, since a process
  group is not a sandbox.

FIX 9 (CHECK 11 — the process-entry inventory was unbounded). CP-04 now carries a
  **closure rule** (the transitive closure of entries reachable through paths the
  profile permits — finite because the permitted path set is finite; anything reachable
  only by an arbitrary absolute path is handled by the confinement claim instead) and a
  **runtime-created-executable rule** (post-admission binaries are `UNKNOWN` by
  construction, and the closure is re-verified whenever the permitted path set
  changes). And the conclusion the reviewer's objection forces, stated rather than
  argued away: **if the profile can execute workspace-resident binaries at all — which
  it must, since `AUTO` runs build tooling — then a static universal proof of
  control-plane unreachability is not available in principle.** CP-04 is therefore
  restated as a **bounded** claim with an explicit residual, and that residual is
  reported to the operator at P0. Three rounds (3, 8, 12) produced three attempts to
  claim the universal form; this is the round that stops trying.

FIX 10 (CHECK 12 — NET-10 was strict with a derived outcome). Its required outcome is
  "same as direct", which is `ASK` in column B, so the strict declaration asserted a
  shape the row does not have. NET-10 is removed from the strict list and its property
  is restated as the **inequality** it actually tests: `grandchild_verdict ≤
  parent_verdict` in every column — which is strictly stronger than a fixed expected
  value, because it holds for any future parent verdict.

FIX 11 (new defect — B0 = A as an identity without the condition). Covered by FIX 5;
  the condition is now a **profile** requirement compiled by P1c and covered by
  criterion 8's gate, with the checkable form: with the proxy running and unbound, a
  child's attempt to reach it must fail, or the column is `UNKNOWN`, not `A`.

FIX 12 (new defect — pidfd listed as sufficient). Covered by FIX 4.

FIX 13 (newly raised — PE-09 needs a mechanism the path profile does not provide).
  A path-deny profile says nothing about an already-open descriptor: the child reads a
  number it inherited, not a path. Denying `~/.ssh/id_rsa` by path leaves an inherited
  fd to that inode fully readable. The vector's note now says so, and — because a note
  alone was exactly the round-12/13 failure mode — **P1a gains
  `INHERITED_DESCRIPTOR_GATE` as a table row**: at `exec`, close the child's
  descriptor table to a named allow-list and set `FD_CLOEXEC` on every internal
  descriptor the runner opens. A backend that cannot rewrite the descriptor table
  makes PE-09 `NOT_ENFORCED` and must say so. The row states why this is a gate and
  not a refinement: without it, `secret.read` is not enforced for the shell surface
  even for enumerated paths.

FIX 14 (newly raised — fetch and push are indistinguishable once a grant exists).
  A column-B grant is issued to a **destination**, not to a Git verb, so an approved
  `git fetch` does not constrain a later `git push` to the same endpoint, and the
  network profile has no view of which verb produced the bytes. **New vector NET-06b**
  records `NOT_ENFORCED` for the in-child case in every column, with the consequences
  stated: `git.remote.write` stays `DENY until P1c ships` and P1b's gate does not make
  it `ALLOW` for a bound destination, and separating the two verbs inside a child would
  require the per-action mediation this plan has now excluded three times.

