ROUND 15 — FIXES
================================================================================

The round-15 verdict is accepted in full. Two of its findings retire a whole class
of claim rather than a single sentence, and they are the reason this round is
recorded at length rather than as a list of edits.

**The finding that recurs, in a fourth distinct form.** Rounds 11, 12 and 14 each
caught the document claiming more than its evidence, and each time the shape was
different: a fix that did not propagate to a second location (r11), a fix that
described a sweep it had not run (r12), a fix that narrowed a claim on a surface
the claim was not about (r14). Round 15's CHECK 10 and CHECK 3 are the same failure
once more, in the two forms not yet seen:

  - CHECK 10 — "tree ownership is **largely present** on both the managed and the
    direct paths." The observation behind it is that **some** direct sites set a
    process group. "Some" was summarized as "largely present on both", which is a
    claim about the *set*, made from evidence about *members*.
  - CHECK 3 — "the logical session id the runner **already mints**." The evidence
    contains no such observation. The only session-keyed grants anywhere in the
    evidence set are **Hermes'** (`H-17`, `H-21`), and those describe a
    third-party project, not this one. A reusable *pattern* was read as a
    statement about WebCodex.

The general form, now stated for rounds 16+: **a claim about a set requires
evidence indexed by the set's members, not evidence about a few of them; and a
mechanism observed in a *reused* component is evidence about that component only.**
Both errors are invisible to a reader who trusts the author, which is why
fifteen rounds of self-repair did not catch either one.

FIX 1 (CHECK 1 — the gate named the wrong phase). `COMPONENT_REUSE_MATRIX.md §2`
  now says the reuse recommendation is gated on **P1b criterion (11)**, which is
  where the two assessments actually live, rather than on P0. The recommendation
  remains provisional, as intended; only the phase reference is corrected.

FIX 2 (CHECK 3 — the session binding was assumed, not established). Two changes,
  in both documents, and neither is a rewording.

  - `REFERENCE_ARCHITECTURE.md §10` replaces "the logical session id the runner
    already mints" with a **two-state table**: `bound` (the host demonstrably mints
    such an id *and* observes that session's end → per-session expiry enforced) and
    `unbound` (**the default** → per-session expiry `NOT_ENFORCED`, TTL only). The
    section now states plainly that the existence of such an id is a
    **prerequisite the design depends on, not an established fact**, cites Hermes'
    `H-17`/`H-21` as the reusable pattern rather than as evidence about WebCodex,
    and records what *is* observed (`W-42`'s session registry and
    session/project validation) separately from what is not (end-of-life
    observation). The "Transition owner" paragraph is scoped to the `bound` state.
  - `IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md P5` criterion 1 drops "The runner already mints such an
    id … so no new identity is introduced" and makes `bound` an acceptance
    requirement: the state must be **demonstrated** before per-session expiry may be
    claimed, and until then MD-09's session-end leg is `NOT_ENFORCED`.

  The default being `unbound` is the substantive choice. `bound` is the design
  anyone would want and the one an author is tempted to assume; making it the
  default would silently degrade an undemonstrated capability to process-lifetime
  scoping, which is the substitution round 13 already rejected. A plan that cannot
  demonstrate the binding now says `NOT_ENFORCED` and carries the disclosure.

FIX 3 (CHECK 4 — a live summary contradicted the permitted design). Two places,
  not one. `REFERENCE_ARCHITECTURE.md §13`'s "route all model-reachable spawns
  through one path" is rewritten as the **two-part attach surface** (managed path
  plus the shared `sandbox::apply` hook), with round 14's correction cited;
  `OSS_RESEARCH_EVIDENCE.md §2.1a W-23`'s "this is the sandbox attach point" is
  already "one of the two sites on the sandbox-attach surface" from the round-14
  fix and is unchanged.

FIX 4 (CHECK 5 — criterion 8 is not satisfiable, and the plan now says so). The
  three mechanisms round 14 offered as sufficient each fail the reviewer's own
  test, and round 15 named why: **endpoint-anchored grants do not stop A from
  relaying B's bytes to that endpoint** (termination says who may connect, not whose
  bytes arrive), and **denying inbound sockets does not by itself rule out a
  workspace or interprocess handoff** (A can read a file B wrote and send it
  onward; the bytes never traverse a socket). So the criterion is not repaired with
  a fourth mechanism. It is **reduced to a finding and declared unsatisfiable within
  this plan**: an OS-sandbox-shaped boundary plus a shared userspace proxy cannot
  provide per-spawn network grants for a workload that can run arbitrary code,
  because the proxy observes connections rather than traffic. What the plan
  delivers instead: DNS `DENY`; literal-IP/localhost rules compiled into the
  per-spawn sandbox network profile (proxy-independent, so `ASK` for these stands);
  hostname authorization `NOT_ENFORCED` and **not delivered**;
  `SECURITY_TEST_VECTORS.md §4` column B is headed "FUTURE-PHASE TARGET";
  and the relay negative-test's fourth leg (A relaying B) is retained as an
  **unmet acceptance criterion** for whatever phase attempts it. A gate that is
  honestly labelled `NOT_ENFORCED` is a gate that does not release, and the plan
  now says that in its own voice rather than implying completion.

FIX 5 (CHECK 6 — the FD gate was a name, not a criterion). `IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md
  P1a` gains **acceptance criterion (5)**: the `INHERITED_DESCRIPTOR_GATE` must be
  **demonstrably narrowing** the descriptor table at `exec` on the target platform
  for every covered spawn path, verified by the same probe PE-09 uses, and a
  backend that cannot perform the closure is recorded as `sandbox_backend = none`
  for the affected paths rather than proceeding. PE-09 is moved out of
  `OUTCOME_STRICT` and is given per-member outcomes: `DENY` where closure is
  demonstrated, `NOT_ENFORCED` where it is not. If closure cannot be shown, P1a
  does not pass, and the affected surfaces' `secret.read` stays `NOT_ENFORCED` as
  a recorded consequence rather than a silent one.

FIX 6 (CHECK 8 — the qualifier had not propagated). `SECURITY_TEST_VECTORS.md §4`
  NET-11 no longer claims listed secrets are "unreachable in the first place"; the
  cell reads "unreachable **by path on the structured surface**", which is what the
  matrix cell says and what §2's post-admission hardlink admission actually
  supports. The claim and its limit now use the same words in both documents.

FIX 7 (CHECK 10 — the set-level claim from member-level evidence). The
  `OSS_RESEARCH_EVIDENCE.md §7` row is rewritten to state what §2.1a actually
  records: tree ownership is present on the managed path and on **some** of the
  direct path, naming the three direct sites that are documented as setting a
  process group (W-23e, W-23f, W-23g) and observing that the remaining non-test
  direct sites are recorded **without** it, including the two model-reachable
  shell paths that matter most (W-23a, W-23b), with W-23h/W-23i unclassified. The
  row now says explicitly that there is **no basis for a claim in either
  direction**, and makes establishing the per-site facts for W-23a…W-23i a P0 work
  item instead of a generalization. Round history: `none` (rounds ≤12) → `partial`
  (13) → narrowed (14) → bounded per-site claim (15).

FIX 8 (CHECK 11 — the universal claim was not reconciled with the bounded one).
  `IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md P0` criterion (1) is restated in CP-04's own terms. The
  criterion no longer requires that "every non-test spawn site" be classified by
  static call-graph analysis — an open-ended shell plus arbitrary code execution
  makes that undeliverable, and CP-04 had already said so while P0 still demanded
  it. The gate P0 ships is the bounded one: every **catalogued** site (the
  transitive closure reachable through paths the sandbox profile permits) carries a
  three-valued verdict; **every non-catalogued site is `UNKNOWN` and is reported as
  such in the gate record**; the closure is re-verified whenever the profile's
  permitted path set changes; and the residual is surfaced to the operator rather
  than resolved in a document. The reviewer's second sentence on this CHECK —
  *a finite permitted path set also does not make its runtime-created executable
  set finite* — is already CP-04's runtime-created-executable rule and is
  preserved, not weakened.

FIX 9 (CHECK 12 — two expectations were not executable). `SECURITY_TEST_VECTORS.md
  §4` NET-10's expected outcome is restated as a **set-containment property of
  profiles** ("the grandchild inherits the spawn's compiled network profile and no
  other … assert the grandchild is denied for each of the parent's denied
  destinations"), because an in-child socket operation never returns to the
  dispatch boundary and therefore has no verdict to compare; the invented
  `grandchild_verdict` quantity is named in the text as the thing that does not
  exist. NET-10 stays out of `OUTCOME_STRICT` because its outcome is a relation,
  not a value. NET-06b's column A is `DENY` rather than `NOT_ENFORCED`, because
  column A denies the hostname fetch the vector presupposes — the premise does not
  arise, which is a different statement from "cannot be observed".

FIX 10 (newly-raised — the network `ASK` had no spawn lifetime). `§6.1(b)` had
  excluded long-lived surfaces from network `ASK` outright, but the §4 table gave
  `ASK` to `git fetch`, `git push` and package installation with no lifetime
  specified, so the same undetermined question appeared in a second place. A single
  uniform precondition now governs **every** column-B `ASK` in that table: the
  action must be a **mediated, short-lived, single-purpose spawn** that the
  dispatcher creates and reaps for that one action, with the compiled profile
  applied at that spawn and no reuse of a running shell, session, job or nested
  agent. Performed in-child, the same action is `NOT_ENFORCED` on the column-B
  mechanism. This is the §6.1(b) rule that column B must satisfy before any of its
  cells may read `ASK` at all, so it constrains the column rather than softening it.

FIX 11 (newly-raised — PE-06's mechanisms were not source-verified). The Linux
  `fs.protected_hardlinks` knob, the absence of a macOS equivalent, SBPL's lack of
  an `st_nlink` predicate, and uid separation as the ownership boundary are
  **platform knowledge, not observations of the WebCodex tree**; the evidence set
  records nothing about link semantics, admission walks, or uid separation, because
  the audit never looked for them. They are now labelled **design hypotheses** that
  must be verified on the target platform before P1b treats any PE-06 cell as an
  enforced outcome, with the same standing as an unexecuted profile. The two cells
  that already read `KNOWN_LIMITATION` (PE-06a on macOS, PE-06a′ without uid
  separation) are the ones whose outcomes depend on an unverified mechanism, and
  verification is named as a P1b deliverable. This is the §8 discipline applied to
  the vector table: a mechanism named in a plan is not a mechanism observed to
  exist.

