ROUND 16 — FIXES
================================================================================

Fifteen rejections; the author accepted every finding. Round 16 is the first round
whose two new defects were introduced by a repair that had *already been reviewed
and accepted in principle*, which is worth naming because it changes what to watch
for from here.

**The lesson of round 16, which supersedes round 15's in one respect and extends it
in another.** Round 15's organizing rule was: *a claim about a set requires evidence
indexed by the set's members; a mechanism observed in a reused component is evidence
about that component only.* Round 16 confirms that rule from a new direction and
adds a second one:

  - **Confirming case.** CHECK 3's second half. Round 15 caught "the runner already
    mints" as an unestablished claim and replaced it with a careful two-state table.
    Round 16 then found that the *replacement's supporting evidence* was the same
    error one level down: `W-42` records error-kind **strings** emitted by a
    classifier, which is evidence the code distinguishes cases, not evidence that a
    registry exists. The repair was honest about the claim and still leaned on an
    over-read of the evidence — because the rule was applied to the headline sentence
    and not to the paragraph underneath it that was supposed to make it credible.
  - **New rule, and the opposite error.** Round 16's first NEW DEFECT is
    `NOT_ENFORCED` applied where enforcement **does** exist. Under the DENY-only
    network profile these surfaces ship with, a socket attempt from inside a child is
    denied by the compiled profile; there is no per-action mediation, but there *is*
    per-socket confinement. Round 15's rule said a narrowing must stay about the same
    thing; round 16 shows the symmetric case — **a widening to `NOT_ENFORCED` is also
    a defect when a mechanism observes the effect**, and it is the same mistake in the
    opposite direction, with the same consequence for a reader: they will believe a
    hole exists where one does not, and design around it. `NOT_ENFORCED` must mean
    "no mechanism observes this", never "I could not construct the observation".

Both rules together are the standard the remaining rounds are held to: **every cell
must be justified by an indexed evidence set, and `NOT_ENFORCED` is a claim about
the world that requires the same justification as `DENY` does.**

FIX 1 (CHECK 3 — strict acceptance vs. the default `unbound` state). MD-09 was
  removed from `OUTCOME_STRICT`, and the strictness it carried was moved to the two
  places that can enforce it, which are deliberately different. In the `bound` state
  the session-end leg is strict. In `unbound` — the default — **the mode is not
  offered at all**, and this is the substantive part: round 15 made `bound` a
  requirement for *claiming per-session expiry* but left the mode offerable anyway
  with TTL-only expiry, so a danger mode whose activation outlives the session that
  requested it was still reachable. A TTL is not a session. `IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md`
  P5 now says `DANGER_FULL_ACCESS` is offered **only** in `bound` and resolves to
  `DENY` in `unbound` — the same deliberate "boring failure direction" P5 criterion 7
  already takes when the `RELAXED_BACKEND` criteria have not passed. TTL remains an
  additional bound in `bound`, never a substitute.

FIX 2 (CHECK 3, second half — the supporting evidence was over-read). Both
  `REFERENCE_ARCHITECTURE.md §10` and `P5` now state that `W-42` records only
  **error-kind strings** produced by a classifier, which is evidence the code
  distinguishes those cases and not evidence that a session registry exists — a
  classifier can name a condition no component currently produces. The earlier
  round-15 text, which cited those strings as showing "a session registry and
  session/project validation already exist", is withdrawn. `bound` is therefore a
  P5 **work item with a named deliverable** (demonstrate a host-minted logical
  session id for the activating request, and an observable end-of-life signal), not a
  formality — and while it is unmet, `unbound` governs.

FIX 3 (CHECK 5 — the release gate was self-defeating, and the plan's delivery claim
  was therefore unreadable). P1c criterion 6 said no network `ASK` may be offered
  until criteria 1–8 pass, while criterion 8 was declared unsatisfiable within the
  plan. Those cannot both hold: the gate could never be satisfied, so it silently
  blocked **every** network `ASK` — including the literal-IP and localhost grants
  that are enforced by the compiled sandbox profile and never touch the proxy. This
  is the reviewer's strongest remaining objection stated exactly right: *a reader
  cannot determine which network capabilities the plan actually releases.* The gate
  is now split by enforcement path:

  - **Proxy path (hostname grants):** criteria 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7 **and 8** —
    unchanged, and since criterion 8 is unsatisfiable here, **no hostname `ASK` is
    ever released by this plan**. That is the accepted consequence, and it is why
    §4 column B is headed FUTURE-PHASE TARGET.
  - **Compiled-profile path (literal-IP / localhost-port, i.e. `NET-02`/`NET-04`):**
    criteria 1, 4, 5 and **7** only. Enforced by the kernel on the child's own
    sockets at spawn time; gated on the rule shape being compiled and negatively
    tested, which is a real and satisfiable gate; **not** gated on criterion 8,
    because there is no proxy in the path and so no attribution problem to solve.

  This is the only network `ASK` the plan delivers, and the distinction is not
  cosmetic — it is exactly the difference between a mechanism that constrains a
  socket and a mechanism that mediates one, which is the distinction criterion 8's
  own finding was about.

FIX 4 (CHECK 5, second half — the "no proxy to relay through" argument). The plan
  defended the profile path by saying a child cannot relay through it because there is
  no proxy to relay through. **The conclusion survives; the reason does not.** A can
  receive B's bytes through the shared workspace — B writes a file, A reads it — and
  send them over A's permitted socket; those bytes never traverse a proxy because
  there is no proxy in the path. A profile governs **which sockets a process may
  open**, not **whose bytes leave through them**. The defensible claim, now the only
  one made, is the narrower one: **B cannot open a destination its own profile
  forbids, and A's grant cannot be widened by B.** Any statement about the *origin* of
  bytes leaving over A's socket is `NOT_ENFORCED`, for the same reason it is on the
  proxy path, and is recorded rather than claimed away.

FIX 5 (new defect 1 — `NOT_ENFORCED` where a mechanism does enforce). The §4
  uniform precondition said the in-child case is `NOT_ENFORCED` "because dispatch
  cannot see the action". That conflated two different things, and the round-16
  reviewer's correction is the sharper statement:

  - **Is the `ASK` grant available for this action?** No — in-child. Only the
    mediated, single-purpose spawn can carry one.
  - **Is the network action itself enforced?** Yes — by the compiled profile. A
    destination the profile denies stays denied regardless of which process inside
    the child attempts it, and grandchild sockets are covered by inheritance.

  The honest statement is *stronger* than the one it replaces (`DENY` by profile),
  and the honest limitation is confined to the grant.

FIX 6 (CHECK 8 — the over-claim was in the note, not the cell). Round 15 narrowed
  NET-11's cell to "unreachable by path on the structured surface" and left the
  adjacent bullet asserting the material "was **never readable** among the listed
  patterns" — dropping the surface qualifier entirely, so the note was *stronger*
  than the cell it was explaining. It is false on the shell surface for the two
  reasons the paragraph above it names: a post-admission hardlink makes the listed
  inode readable under another name, and a pre-`exec` inherited descriptor is not a
  path at all. The surviving claim is the asymmetric one, stated plainly: path-denied
  on the structured surface; `NOT_ENFORCED` on the shell surface for a
  post-admission alias or an inherited descriptor. The consequence is now written out
  rather than left to be inferred — a narrow `IP:port` grant plus a shell that can
  read a listed secret by alias **is** a working exfiltration path, and the network
  axis is not what stops it.

FIX 7 (CHECK 11 — the universal claim survived in two more places). P0's bounded
  restatement was correct, and `CP-01` still demanded "every non-test spawn site"
  while `CP-04` still labelled control-plane sites "**proven** unreachable" — the
  same universal claim CP-04's own second consequence had abandoned, so the two
  halves of one gate said different things about what passing means. `CP-01` now
  enumerates the **catalogued** sites with a three-valued verdict and records
  non-catalogued sites as `UNKNOWN` **in the gate record rather than omitted from
  it**. `CP-04`'s cell drops "proven" and the pass condition is split: for a
  catalogued site, all paths `UNREACHABLE`; for a non-catalogued site, `UNKNOWN` by
  construction and **no pass claimed**.

FIX 8 (CHECK 12 — the table still said "same as direct"). NET-10's explanatory note
  was repaired in round 15 while the **table row** kept "same as direct" in columns
  where the direct outcome is a dispatch-time `ASK` — the same cell the note said has
  no verdict to inherit. The row now reads "profile containment, not a verdict", with
  the relation named and the "same as direct" wording explicitly withdrawn. NET-10
  stays out of `OUTCOME_STRICT` because its outcome is a relation, not a value.

FIX 9 (newly raised 1 — PE-06a's Linux `DENY` was unconditional). `fs.protected_hardlinks`
  is not a pure ownership test: the kernel also permits the link when the process
  wants to read *and write* the file, so the rule is closer to "denied unless the
  linker could already open the source read-write" — stronger than the cell claimed
  for a read-only source and **weaker** for a source the agent may already write. The
  cell asserted `DENY` on a precondition stated as if sufficient, while round 15's
  provenance note labelled the whole mechanism unverified platform knowledge.
  Corrected: PE-06a is `KNOWN_LIMITATION` on **both** platforms until the exact
  kernel condition is reproduced on the target kernel; `DENY` only afterwards; macOS
  has no mechanism at all. P1b's deliverable includes reproducing the three cases
  (source owned / not owned / writable). The member table, the mechanism table and
  the family summary row are all updated, so no `DENY` cell for PE-06a survives
  anywhere as an enforced outcome.

FIX 10 (newly raised 2 — a denial of *pending* requests cited as revocation of an
  *issued* grant). `REFERENCE_ARCHITECTURE.md §9` cited OpenCode's session finalizer
  (`permission.ts:119-129`, cascading `reject` to pending requests) as support for
  "session grants die with the session". The observation establishes only that
  **unanswered requests are denied** at session end; it says nothing about revoking a
  grant that was already issued. The correct reference for that property is Hermes'
  `tools/approval.py:285` `clear_session(session_key)`, which is already in the
  evidence set. Both are now named, with the distinction stated, because the gap they
  leave is real: with no revoke path, a grant issued near a session's end survives
  into a state the design assumes cannot occur.

