ROOT CAUSE OF G1 — the finding that matters more than the finding it caused
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
`f26a.py` computed the RA §3 corrections (B2's present-state row AND the
re-derived summary sentence) against an in-memory string and then fell off the
end of the script **without a write-back call**. It printed "OK  CHECK 3" and
"OK  CHECK 3 (cont.)". The stdout log recorded success. The document on disk was
never touched — mtime evidence: `REFERENCE_ARCHITECTURE.md` 12:46:35, the script
12:54:47.

This is a violation of the evidence-authenticity invariant, and it is
self-inflicted: the round-25 log entry, written after the fact, asserts "B2's row
reads `UNVERIFIED`; the summary beneath the table matches the seven corrected
rows." That assertion was **false when written**. A log entry describing an
intent or a computed-but-unpersisted edit is the same category as a fabricated
observation, and the mechanism that produced it — a fix script that reports
success it did not achieve — is the one most able to escape notice, because the
log looks like evidence.

The lesson is therefore not "re-apply the fix". It is that **every write in a
fix script must be followed by a read-back assertion against the file**, so a
dropped write fails in the round that made it instead of the round that audits
it. `f27a.py`/`f27b.py` adopt this as a `save()` helper that re-reads the file
and asserts the mutation string is present; the 2026-09-29 run of f27a/f27b
recorded nine `[verified on disk]` confirmations.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
LESSON LEDGER (continued)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
L43. **A fix script that does not write back is not a fix; assert the write.**
    Success printed to stdout, and success recorded in a review log, are both
    downstream of the same unverified assumption. Re-read after every write.
L44. **A review log entry is a claim about the artifact, not about the session.**
    Writing "B2's row reads UNVERIFIED" after running a script that did not
    persist that edit manufactures a false record. The log must be written
    against the file, in the same act as verifying it.
L45. **Remove a disjunction when one branch cannot produce the report.** P1a(1c)
    read "a runtime hook, **or** a CI check". The "or" is what created the
    defect: any disjunction invites a reader to take the satisfiable branch, and
    the reviewer's job is to notice that one branch is categorically different in
    kind (source inspection vs runtime observation) rather than in degree.
L46. **A gate must have exactly one definition of "passes" everywhere it is
    named.** (10c) and (N-a) disagreed about what satisfying criterion (10) means;
    (N-c) and the compiled-profile sub-bullet disagreed about the criterion set.
    This is the third recurrence of the round-20 criterion-6 failure and confirms
    it as a structural habit, not an accident: **when a gate is restated in a
    new place, the restatement must be derived from the first statement, not
    retyped.**
L47. **"covered by X" in a gap table is not "delivered by X".** `OSS §7` listed
    the OS sandboxes as `Gap? none` because Codex has a route, while `§8` records
    that no profile was ever compiled. Coverage tables must distinguish
    *route identified* from *mechanism established*, exactly as the disposition
    table now distinguishes `SPECIFIED` from `DELIVERED`.

