Round 27's prompt footer told the reviewer "The submission's review log is part
of the material above" and the LOG-TRUTH AUDIT instructed it to audit the log's
mechanical-sweep figures. The assembly command was:

    cat part0-head.md <6 documents> > round27-prompt.txt
    cat verify.py >> ...
    cat round27-footer.md >> round27-prompt.txt

`review/codex-architecture-review.log` — 4,458 lines, 308,123 bytes at the time —
was never in the `cat` list. So the submission named a deliverable in its own
instructions and did not deliver it, and the reviewer correctly refused to
audit a document it did not have.

This is L24 (a deliverable that is named but has no submission is a promise), and
it is the THIRD instance in this review — after the P1b(12) allow-list gate and
the EX-13/EX-14 disposition. The pattern is now unmistakable and is stated as
such in L48: **this review's dominant failure mode is not writing a wrong claim
but promising an artifact it never delivers.** Every one of the three instances
was introduced while fixing an earlier round's finding about a missing gate.

Round 28's assembly is therefore verified before the review runs: the log is
concatenated, and its byte count and line count are printed by the assembly step
itself so that a reviewer can check the claim against the prompt it is reading.
The fix is in the assembly, because the defect was never in the prose.

