THE STRUCTURAL FINDING, STATED AS A RULE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Five of round 28's nine blocking findings — CHECKS 2, 5, 6, 7, 9 — are ONE
defect: this review appends a correction next to a claim instead of replacing the
claim, and the correction then lives inside the same operative cell.

Round 28 said so itself about STV §4 ("calling a later sentence its withdrawal
does not give an implementer one unambiguous disposition") and then found the same
ordering in the matrix's Default cell, P1c(10c), P1c's opening gate, and
NET-06b1's outcome column. It is the same shape as L11 (a repair beside a claim
is not a repair), one level up: **a repair beside the strong claim leaves the
strong claim in the position a reader reads first.**

The round-29 rule is therefore positional, not editorial: **the operative cell
must open with the weak claim, and the withdrawn text must live outside it.**

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
LESSON LEDGER (continued)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
L52. **Report bytes and characters separately, and never label one as the
    other.** Python's `len()` on a decoded string is characters; `wc -c` and
    `os.path.getsize` are bytes. They differ for every file containing an em dash
    or a section sign, which is every document in this submission. Round 28
    published character counts in a column labelled bytes and the reviewer caught
    it in one line. **Publish a sha256 prefix as well -- and round 30 corrects what
    that buys: a count can be compared, and a hash can be compared *if the
    reader recomputes it against the original bytes*. A hash in a self-published
    manifest is a convenience for locating a file, not evidence that the bytes
    submitted are the bytes described** (L61; round 29 CHECK 11).
L53. **A test expectation cannot be a statement about frequency.** NET-06b1 said
    `DENY` in the outcome column and "in the ordinary case" in the prose. A test
    column asserts the property holds; if the property is best-effort, the
    honest expectation is that the row fails on a machine where the best-effort
    misses.
L54. **A gate that cannot be met is not an open risk; it is an unsatisfiable
    plan.** P3(7b) required a test of a boundary no mechanism produces. That does
    not make the boundary unverified, it makes the phase unshippable. When a
    criterion's producer does not exist, the fix is to move the RELEASE GATE to a
    criterion the plan can meet (here: withhold host-UI approval), and keep the
    original as a specification of what must eventually be built.
L55. **Selecting a producer does not establish what the producer can see.**
    Round 28 removed "three unselected mechanisms" and installed one, then
    attached a coverage claim the mechanism does not support. The selection fixed
    the producer gap and opened a coverage gap. **A narrower choice needs a
    narrower claim, and both must be written in the same edit.**
L56. **A known false positive that is documented but not fixed is worse than one
    that is neither, because the documentation reads as a fix.** FP3 (criterion
    numbers are phase-local) was recorded in the verifier's header in 2025 as
    "corrected". It was not corrected for four rounds. Round 28's reviewer read
    the code and found the document-wide pool still in place; round 29 attempted
    the fix and a negative control showed the new implementation was also dead.
    **Two rounds of documentation were worse than none**, because each one moved
    the item from "known defect" to "handled" in the reader's mind while the
    behaviour was unchanged.
L57. **A negative control is the only honest test of a checker, and a checker
    that fails its negative control must be disabled rather than reported.**
    Round 29 injected `criterion (78)` where `criterion (9)` had been and the
    verifier passed. The response was to mark the check NOT A GATE and state the
    failing control in the artifact, not to tune the regex until the control
    passed. Tuning until a control passes is how a checker learns to accept the
    one input you tested and nothing else. **A disabled check that says why is
    more useful than an enabled check that does not work, because the second
    failure mode is invisible.**

