--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE DECISION L58 ASKED FOR
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
L58 (written at the end of round 29) named four options: narrow the gate, split
the phase, defer the boundary, or stop calling the plan minimal and executable.
Round 30 takes the first two, and does not take the fourth, because the
characterisation it would remove is not the thing that is wrong.

  TAKEN: split the phase and narrow the gate.
    - P1a (1c) leaves P1a's acceptance set and becomes phase **P1a-R**.
    - P3's two pass conditions become one, and the other becomes phase **P3-R**.

  NOT TAKEN: "stop calling the plan minimal and executable".
    The word "executable" never appeared in the plan document; the reviewer's
    phrase was a description, not a quotation, so there is nothing to withdraw.
    What IS withdrawn is the matrix's "defensible" (CHECK 1), which was a real
    comparative claim about two options nobody had assessed.

WHY SPLITTING IS THE CONSERVATIVE DIRECTION, AND NOT A RELAXATION
  Both new phases have disposition `SPECIFIED`: a criterion exists, no producer
  is designed, nothing observes it, and **no phase waits on it**. Nothing became
  weaker. What changed is that the plan no longer claims two phases are
  unfinished-but-on-track when they are, in fact, ungated. The residual
  questions -- can a child bypass the audited spawn API; can a model drive the
  host UI that shows an approval -- remain open, remain named, and now cannot be
  mistaken for work already scheduled behind a gate.

