  (1b) alone**." Round 30 CHECK 4: "P1a (1c) still says ... 'P1a does not pass on
  (1b) alone.' The later P1a-R section says precisely that P1a passes on (1a) and
  (1b)." **The correction sat after the claim.**

  F30d rewrote CP-07's tail and STRUCK OUT its second clause -- while the FIRST
  clause, "P3 does not pass while CP-07 is NOT_ENFORCED", stayed live. Round 30
  CHECK 3: "CP-07 also retains the live sentence ... whereas P3-R says CP-07
  does not block P3." **A live clause outranks a struck one, and striking the
  wrong one is worse than not striking either.**

  F30e added "recommended as the cheapest assessed option" to matrix §2's
  closing characterisation and left "SUBPROCESS_ADAPTER **wins** on the assessed
  set by a margin the unassessed options are unlikely to close" standing in the
  numbered item above it. Round 30 CHECK 1 called that "the comparative claim
  the section says it withdrew."

This is L59 committed by the author of L59. The rule was stated correctly, was
applied by hand to the sentences under discussion, and the sentences NOT under
discussion kept their claims. **Round 31 therefore is not new analysis: it is the
positional discipline applied to the positions I had missed.**

