L68. **Marking a sentence withdrawn does not withdraw it.** Round 31's four
    blocking fixes were all the same error: the superseded claim stood at its
    own position and the correction was written behind it, so a reader scanning
    for the operative statement found the old claim. `~~struck~~` is a claim
    about the author's intent, not about the text's meaning. **The rule that
    actually works: delete or replace the superseded sentence where it stands,
    and quote it in the correction so a reader who remembers it can find the
    replacement.**
L69. **The author of a rule is the likeliest person to break it.** L59 was
    written in round 29, and round 30 -- the very next round -- committed it in
    four places while believing it was applying it. A rule stated in a ledger is
    not a rule applied; the only evidence is a mechanical sweep over the CLASS,
    and "I applied it to the sentences under discussion" is a statement about
    attention, not coverage.
L70. **A check that previews less than it asserts is a check that lies by
    truncation.** The strict-list check printed a 96-character preview of each
    membership declaration and was read as a membership audit while matching a
    single line. Three verifier findings in this review have the same shape:
    the criterion-reference check, the preview, and the byte/char manifest. **In
    all three the output format, not the logic, was what made the claim
    stronger than the check.**

