--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
LESSON LEDGER (continued)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
L71. **When one object is described two ways, no editorial operation separates the
    descriptions; only producing the property or removing the feature does.** P3
    claimed its confirmation UI was unreachable and unmeasurable in the same
    cell. Round 31 treated it as a wording problem and produced a second wording
    problem. **The test for whether a contradiction is editorial or structural: if
    both claims are about the SAME object rather than about different objects,
    it is structural.** P3's two claims were about one dialog; the earlier P3/CP-07
    conflict was about two different gates, and that one was editorial.
L72. **A contradiction between a phase and its downstream consumers is repaired
    at the consumer, not only at the phase.** Removing human approval from P3
    required editing P5's criterion (5), P5's ROLLBACK, AR-03, and the
    architecture's flow diagram, because each of them encoded "escalation reaches
    a person". **Any scope reduction must be swept for every consumer of the
    thing reduced** -- the same class as L59, one level up: L59 was about claims
    in the same cell, L72 is about claims in other documents.
L73. **Some questions are not decidable by the tool that has been running for
    thirty-two rounds.** CHECK 12 asks the lexical sweep to arbitrate whether a
    phase may claim a property. It cannot, and the useful response is to say so
    rather than to add checks that gesture at the question. **The sweep's value is
    catching the mechanical residue of a design decision, never making the
    decision.**

